From 1c48164446ce10e55a1bb87cc6dea0f530b89d91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 20:02:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Split Inkling 2's layers over two cards by its prompt
 pipeline

The split by bytes put layers 0-13 on one card and 14-23 on the
other: with the core (6-17) visited three times, 30 of the 48 visits
ran on the first card, busy 11.7 s of a 13 s prompt read while the
second waited 40% of it. Now:

- with two cards, card 0 takes layers [0, x) and [y, z) (the last
  layer on card 1): of the splits whose two-executor prompt pipeline
  (chunk c's visit waits for chunk c - 1's) is within 5% of the
  fastest, the one leaving both cards the closest free VRAM. For
  Inkling-2-Mini: 0-5 and 12-18 on GPU 0, 6-11 and 19-23 on GPU 1,
  every hand-off about as long;
- the head (and the DSpark drafter beside it, its file size counted)
  goes on either card, wherever it evens the free VRAM;
- each card's layers start with the experts that fit on that card;
- a prompt read of 4096+ tokens (and 1/16 of what the window holds:
  the flip copies the window from RAM twice) reads whole windows, not
  ones split across the cards;
- the load line lists each card's layer runs and the head's card.

The same products (the 20K text's ppl unchanged). Both executors,
the drafter: 7K 1825 -> 1971 tok/s, 60K 1867 -> 2222, 180K 1509 ->
1782; free VRAM after start 953/167 -> 529/559 MiB, so no experts go
to RAM for the second executor at 32K-512K; a 4-row verify at 7K 80
-> 35 ms, decode otherwise unchanged.
